<p>
<b><font size=4>TURBIDITY TEST</font></b><br>
As the Muddy Waters Flow
<p>
<b>Peter Goodwin, Gloria Beattie, and Dave Fuhrman</b><br>
<i>University of Idaho (UI), Boise</i>
<p>
This test was truly a team effort and ran on June 23rd - 24th.  If you recall, the purpose of this test was to develop contingency plans for managing turbidity during the next construction season.  The preliminary results are encouraging and show a significant reduction in turbidity and suspended sediment when turbid water was discharged across the floodplain.  The full results and interpretation of the test will be presented at the TAC meeting in September. The consultant team would like to acknowledge and thank the USDA ARS Northwest Watershed Research Center in Boise for loaning the gauging weirs and the truck for transporting this bulky equipment from Boise to Red River.
Specific tasks completed were:
<ol>
<li>Rent and install a 4" pump.
<li>Install the gauging weirs to create a closed flow path from the wetland area (constructed last year) through an old meander channel and then back into the wetland area.  No turbid water was discharged into the river in this test (refer to memorandum describing the test, dated June 14, 1998).
<li>Run tests at three different discharge rates to evaluate the reduction in turbidity between the upstream and downstream ends of the test channel.
<li>Perform a detailed survey of the test site so that velocities and other flow characteristics can be estimated along different flow paths at the test site.
<li>Remove the weirs and other monitoring equipment used for the test.
</ol>
